---
import RevealLayout from "../../layouts/PresentationLayout.astro";
import {
  Probador,
  GarmentIcon,
  ParenthesesCounter,
  DEFAULT_GARMENT_COLORS,
} from "../../components/cave/you-cannot-take-off-a-jacket-you-never-wore";
import RevealCode from "../../components/RevealCode";
---

<RevealLayout
  title="You Can't Take Off a Jacket You Never Wore"
  description="Solving balanced parentheses with a dressing room metaphor"
  theme="dark"
>
  <section>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full gap-8">
      <!--
      It might sound odd at first, but there is a close relationship between trying on
      clothes and knowing if a sequence of parentheses is valid.

      The first sequence is valid. The second one is not.
      -->
      <div class="grid grid-rows-2 gap-40 text-center">
        <div class="fragment fade-in grid grid-cols-2 items-center gap-4">
          <code class="text-6xl tracking-wider">
            <span class="text-green-200">(</span>
            <span class="text-green-200">(</span>
            <span class="text-green-200">)</span>
            <span class="text-green-200">)</span>
          </code>
          <div class="text-6xl">✅</div>
        </div>
        <div class="fragment fade-in grid grid-cols-2 items-center gap-4">
          <code class="text-6xl tracking-wider">
            <span class="text-red-200">(</span>
            <span class="text-red-200">)</span>
            <span
              class="text-red-200 line-through decoration-red-500 decoration-4"
              >)</span
            >
          </code>
          <div class="text-6xl">❌</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <!--
    We are given a line of parentheses. The question is simple: is this line well
    formed? Every opening parenthesis must be closed. And nothing should close
    before it's opened.

    The first thing we tried was counting. Parentheses come in pairs. So we count
    how many open, and how many close.

    If the numbers match... maybe we are done. And in many cases, we are.
    And when the numbers do not match, the sequence is clearly invalid.

    If the number of opening parentheses is greater than the number of closing
    parentheses, something is missing.
    If the number of closing parentheses is greater than the number of opening
    parentheses, something is extra.
    -->
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full">
      <ParenthesesCounter
        client:load
        mode="count"
        title="Count Opening & Closing"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <!--
    But then there is this.
    )(
    Same count. Nothing missing. Nothing extra. And still, completely broken. So
    counting helps. But it doesn't tell the whole story. Order matters.

    So instead of counting everything at the end, we follow the line as it unfolds.
    We keep track of how many opening parentheses we have seen that are still
    pending to be closed.

    If we try to close more parentheses than we have opened at any point, we know
    the sequence is invalid.
    -->
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full">
      <ParenthesesCounter
        client:load
        mode="balance"
        title="Tracking Pending to Close"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <!--
    This works well for a single type of parenthesis. But what if we have more than
    one type? We have parentheses, brackets, and curly braces.

    It might seem that it works the same way. But look at this one.
    (]
    So order matters, and type matters.

    We can try to keep track of how many opening parentheses of each type we have
    seen that are still pending to be closed.

    (]
    This example is no longer a problem. However, it's easy to find a new issue.

    ([)]
    We are trying to close a parenthesis when there is still an unclosed bracket. So
    we need to remember the most recent unclosed opener.
    -->
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full">
      <ParenthesesCounter
        client:load
        mode="multi-count"
        title="Counter Per Type"
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <!--
    Let's use an analogy to make this idea clearer. Think of a changing room. You
    walk in, and you put one t-shirt on. Then a sweater. Then a jacket.

    After looking at yourself in the mirror, you start taking your clothes off.

    You have to take off the jacket first. Then the sweater. Then the t-shirt.
    -->
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full gap-6">
      <h2 class="text-4xl font-bold">The Dressing Room Analogy</h2>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-3 gap-12 mt-8 text-center">
        <div class="fragment flex flex-col items-center">
          <GarmentIcon
            client:load
            type="T"
            color={DEFAULT_GARMENT_COLORS.T}
            size={100}
            showLabel={true}
          />
          <p class="text-xl mt-4">T-Shirt</p>
        </div>
        <div class="fragment flex flex-col items-center">
          <GarmentIcon
            client:load
            type="S"
            color={DEFAULT_GARMENT_COLORS.S}
            size={100}
            showLabel={true}
          />
          <p class="text-xl mt-4">Sweater</p>
        </div>
        <div class="fragment flex flex-col items-center">
          <GarmentIcon
            client:load
            type="J"
            color={DEFAULT_GARMENT_COLORS.J}
            size={100}
            showLabel={true}
          />
          <p class="text-xl mt-4">Jacket</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Slide 11: Valid nested sequence -->
  <section>
    <!--
    It's not possible to take off the sweater before the jacket. The last thing you
    put on is the first thing you take off.
    -->
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full">
      <Probador
        client:load
        actions={[
          { type: "PUT", garment: "T" },
          { type: "PUT", garment: "S" },
          { type: "PUT", garment: "J" },
          { type: "TAKE_OFF", garment: "J" },
          { type: "TAKE_OFF", garment: "S" },
          { type: "TAKE_OFF", garment: "T" },
        ]}
        displayMode="stack"
        showTypeCounters={false}
        autoPlay={false}
        showControls={true}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Slide 12: Stack insight -->
  <section>
    <!--
    This is exactly how our problem works. Instead of stacking clothes one on top of
    another, we are stacking opening parentheses. When we see a closing parenthesis,
    we need to make sure it matches the most recent opening parenthesis we have
    seen.
    -->
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full gap-6">
      <p class="text-2xl text-gray-300">
        With multiple types, we need to remember
      </p>
      <p class="text-4xl font-bold text-amber-400">WHAT was put on last</p>
      <p class="text-2xl text-gray-300 fragment mt-4">Not just count!</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Slide 13: The tricky example intro -->
  <section>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full gap-6">
      <h2 class="text-4xl font-bold">The Tricky Example</h2>
      <p class="text-2xl text-gray-300 fragment">
        The <strong>count per type</strong> might look balanced...
      </p>
      <p class="text-2xl text-red-400 fragment">
        ...but the sequence is <strong>impossible</strong>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Slide 14: Tricky example demo -->
  <section>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full">
      <Probador
        client:load
        actions={[
          { type: "PUT", garment: "T" },
          { type: "PUT", garment: "S" },
          { type: "TAKE_OFF", garment: "T" },
        ]}
        displayMode="both"
        showTypeCounters={true}
        autoPlay={false}
        showControls={true}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Slide 15: Tricky insight -->
  <section>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full gap-6">
      <div class="text-6xl">❌</div>
      <p class="text-2xl text-gray-300">
        You can't remove the <strong>T-Shirt</strong> when the <strong
          >Sweater</strong
        > is on top!
      </p>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-400 fragment mt-4">
        The counters say everything is fine (1 T, 1 S on)
      </p>
      <p class="text-3xl font-bold text-amber-400 fragment">ORDER MATTERS</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Slide 16: The reveal intro -->
  <section>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full gap-6">
      <h2 class="text-4xl font-bold">The Final Reveal</h2>
      <p class="text-2xl text-gray-300 fragment">
        Watch the labels transform...
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Slide 17: Parentheses reveal -->
  <section>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full">
      <Probador
        client:load
        actions={[
          { type: "PUT", garment: "T" },
          { type: "PUT", garment: "S" },
          { type: "PUT", garment: "J" },
          { type: "TAKE_OFF", garment: "J" },
          { type: "TAKE_OFF", garment: "S" },
          { type: "TAKE_OFF", garment: "T" },
        ]}
        displayMode="stack"
        showParentheses={true}
        autoPlay={false}
        showControls={true}
      />
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Slide 18: Mapping explanation -->
  <section>
    <!--
    Next, we define which closing parenthesis belongs to which opening one. Each
    closer knows exactly what it expects in the stack.
    -->
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full gap-6">
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mb-4">The Mapping</h2>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-x-12 gap-y-4 text-2xl">
        <div class="text-right">
          <code class="text-green-400">(</code> = PUT T-Shirt
        </div>
        <div><code class="text-green-400">)</code> = TAKE OFF T-Shirt</div>
        <div class="text-right fragment">
          <code class="text-blue-400">[</code> = PUT Sweater
        </div>
        <div class="fragment">
          <code class="text-blue-400">]</code> = TAKE OFF Sweater
        </div>
        <div class="text-right fragment">
          <code class="text-purple-400">{`{`}</code> = PUT Jacket
        </div>
        <div class="fragment">
          <code class="text-purple-400">{`}`}</code> = TAKE OFF Jacket
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Slide 19: The insight -->
  <section>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full gap-8">
      <blockquote
        class="text-2xl text-gray-300 max-w-3xl text-center italic border-l-4 border-amber-400 pl-6"
      >
        "Balancing parentheses is exactly like getting dressed in layers without
        breaking the laws of physics."
      </blockquote>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section id="code" class="text-lg">
    <!--
    Let's see how this works in Python code.

    We start with an empty stack. This is where we keep the parentheses that are
    still open. In Python, we implement the stack as a list.

    Now we go through the input line, one character at a time.

    If the character is an opening parenthesis, we push it onto the stack. We are
    just remembering it.

    If the character is not an opening parenthesis, we assume it is a closing one.
    We assume no letters or numbers will appear in the input.

    Do we have something to close? If the stack is empty, we stop immediately.

    If there is something to close, we take the last thing we opened. We use pop to
    get and remove the last item from the stack.

    Now we check if the types match. If they do not, the sequence is invalid. We use
    the matching dictionary to see what we expected to find in the stack, based on
    the closing parenthesis we encountered.

    We repeat this until we reach the end of the line. At the very end, only one
    thing matters. Did we close everything we opened?
    -->
    <RevealCode
      client:only="react"
      animation="fade"
      code={`def is_valid_parentheses(text: str) -> bool:
    # STEP: We create empty stack to remember opening parentheses
    stack = []
    # STEP: Mapping of closing to opening parentheses
    matching = {
        ')': '(',
        ']': '[',
        '}': '{',
    }

    # STEP: Process each character in the input text
    for char in text:
        # STEP: If we see an opening parenthesis, we remember it
        if char in '([{':
            stack.append(char)
        # STEP: If we see a closing parenthesis
        else:  # we assume char in ')]}'
            # STEP: There must be something to close
            if not stack:
                return False

            # STEP: It must close the last opened parenthesis
            last_open = stack.pop()

            # STEP: And it must be the correct type
            if matching[char] != last_open:
                return False

    # STEP: At the end, nothing should be left open
    return len(stack) == 0`}
      `
    />
  </section>
  <section id="followup-code" class="text-lg">
    <RevealCode
      client:only="react"
      animation="fade"
      code={`def is_valid_parentheses(text: str) -> bool:
    stack = []

    for char in text:
        if char in "([{":
            stack.append(char)
        else:
            if not stack:
                return False

            last_open = stack.pop()

            diff = ord(char) - ord(last_open)
            if diff != 1 and diff != 2:
                return False

    return len(stack) == 0
`}
    />
  </section>
  <!-- Slide 20: Counter vs Stack -->
  <section>
    <div class="flex flex-col items-center justify-center h-full gap-8">
      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold">Counter vs Stack</h2>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-2 gap-50 text-xl mt-4">
        <div class="text-center fragment">
          <p class="text-amber-400 font-bold text-2xl mb-4">Single type</p>
          <p class="text-gray-300">Just count!</p>
          <code class="text-green-400 text-3xl block mt-4">( ( ) ( ) )</code>
        </div>
        <div class="text-center fragment">
          <p class="text-amber-400 font-bold text-2xl mb-4">Multiple types</p>
          <p class="text-gray-300">Need a stack!</p>
          <code class="text-3xl block mt-4">
            <span class="text-green-400">(</span>
            <span class="text-blue-400">[</span>
            <span class="text-purple-400">{`{`}</span>
            <span class="text-purple-400">{`}`}</span>
            <span class="text-blue-400">]</span>
            <span class="text-green-400">)</span>
          </code>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <iframe
      src="https://cheesebytes.com/cave/parentheses-balancing/"
      width="1080"
      height="720"></iframe>
  </section>
</RevealLayout>
